<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> skiing is usually easier to start, and snowboarding is often easier to get good at once you're past the first few days. If you want to cruise green runs quickly on a short trip, ski. If you're willing to fall a lot early, or you skateboard or surf, snowboarding is a great choice.</p></div>

<h2>The learning curve</h2>
<p>The classic saying is that skiing is easy to learn and hard to master, while snowboarding is hard to learn and easier to master. It's a generalization, but it holds up for many people.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Skiing:</strong> your legs work independently, and the wedge (pizza) gives you an intuitive way to slow down. Many beginners are riding a lift and making turns on day one.</li>
  <li><strong>Snowboarding:</strong> both feet are locked to one board, and turning means switching between your heel and toe edges. The first day or two usually include lots of falls. Once it clicks, progress often comes quickly.</li>
</ul>
<p>Learn more in the beginner guides for <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %> and <%= link_to "how to snowboard", guide_path("how-to-snowboard") %>.</p>

<h2>Comfort and getting around</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Boots:</strong> ski boots are stiff and awkward to walk in. Snowboard boots are softer and more comfortable off the slopes.</li>
  <li><strong>Lifts and flats:</strong> skiers glide across flat areas and lift lines easily with poles. Snowboarders unstrap one foot and push, which can be tiring on flat runs.</li>
  <li><strong>Carrying gear:</strong> skis, poles, and boots are bulkier to carry than a board.</li>
  <li><strong>Sitting down:</strong> snowboarders sit to strap in, so waterproof pants matter more.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Injuries and safety</h2>
<p>The two sports tend to injure different body parts. Skiers more often hurt their knees, because the legs can twist independently. Snowboarders more often injure wrists and shoulders from falling onto outstretched hands, especially as beginners. A helmet is standard for both, and wrist guards are worth it for new snowboarders.</p>

<h2>Terrain</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Powder:</strong> snowboards float well in deep snow, and many riders find powder easier on a board.</li>
  <li><strong>Moguls and steep, tight terrain:</strong> skiing usually has the edge.</li>
  <li><strong>Terrain park:</strong> both are popular; snowboarding has a strong park culture.</li>
  <li><strong>Flat traverses and cat tracks:</strong> skiers have a clear advantage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cost</h2>
<p>Costs are similar. Lift tickets and lessons cost the same either way, and rental prices for a ski setup and a board setup are usually comparable. If you buy, skis need poles; snowboards don't. See <%= link_to "should you rent or buy", guide_path("rent-or-buy-ski-gear") %> before spending money on either.</p>

<h2>Your first day on each</h2>
<h3>A typical first ski day</h3>
<p>You'll learn to walk in boots, glide on flat snow, stop with a wedge, and make wedge turns on a gentle slope. Many beginners ride the beginner chairlift by the afternoon. The challenge comes later, when moving from wedge turns to parallel skiing on steeper runs.</p>
<h3>A typical first snowboard day</h3>
<p>You'll learn to strap in, stand up, and slide on your heel edge and toe edge. Expect plenty of falls, especially when switching edges. Sore wrists and a sore backside are common. By day two or three, many people start linking turns, and progress from there tends to feel fast.</p>

<h2>Gear differences</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Skis:</strong> sized mostly by height and ability. See the <%= link_to "ski size calculator", ski_size_chart_path %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Snowboards:</strong> sized by height, weight, and boot size, with width mattering for bigger feet. See the <%= link_to "snowboard size calculator", snowboard_size_chart_path %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Clothing:</strong> the same layering system works for both, though snowboarders benefit from extra-waterproof pants and padded shorts early on.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to choose</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Short trip, want to cover terrain fast:</strong> ski.</li>
  <li><strong>Skateboard, surf, or wakeboard already:</strong> snowboarding will feel more natural.</li>
  <li><strong>Skiing with family or friends who ski:</strong> matching them makes lessons and lifts easier.</li>
  <li><strong>Bad knees:</strong> talk to a doctor; some people with knee issues find snowboarding easier on their knees, others don't.</li>
  <li><strong>Still undecided:</strong> take a lesson in each on separate days. Many resorts' first-timer packages make that easy.</li>
</ul>
